## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to blend BFS intuition with shortest-path techniques under a hop constraint and to reconstruct a path from augmented state.

## Key Concepts
- Augmented state graph: (node, hops)
- Dijkstra with 2D dist and predecessor tracking
- Path reconstruction from (node, hops) chain
- Time/space: O((N*H + M*H) log(N*H))

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathWithHops(graph, S, T, H) {
  // graph: {u: [{to,w}, ...], ...}
  const dist = new Map();
  const prev = new Map();
  // min-heap assumed available: push({time, node, hops})
  const heap = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.time-b.time);
  dist.set(`${S}|0`, 0);
  heap.push({time:0, node:S, hops:0});
  while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    const {time,u,k} = heap.pop();
    if (time !== dist.get(`${u}|${k}`)) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of (graph[u] || [])) {
      const nk = k + 1;
      if (nk > H) continue;
      const nd = time + w;
      const key = `${v}|${nk}`;
      if (nd < (dist.get(key) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(key, nd);
        prev.set(key, `${u}|${k}`);
        heap.push({time: nd, node: v, hops: nk});
      }
    }
  }
  let bestTime = Infinity; let bestKey = null;
  for (let k = 0; k <= H; k++) {
    const key = `${T}|${k}`;
    const d = dist.get(key);
    if (d != null && d < bestTime) { bestTime = d; bestKey = key; }
  }
  const path = [];
  if (bestKey) {
    let cur = bestKey;
    while (cur) {
      const [node] = cur.split('|');
      path.push(node);
      cur = prev.get(cur);
    }
    path.reverse();
  }
  return { time: bestTime, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple equal shortest paths?
- How would you optimize memory for very large graphs?
- How would you adapt if hops constraint is on edges vs. nodes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple target nodes efficiently?
- How does the solution change if edges can have zero weight, or if counts exceed 64-bit, and you need a different modulus or exact arithmetic?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a hybrid tree-based structure enabling prefix-constrained top-k queries with dynamic weights, balancing time and memory.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node BST for subtree words by (freq, word)
- Global hash map for current frequencies
- Incremental updates propagate along word path; queryTopK uses node BST to fetch top-k quickly
- Trade-offs: memory factor for storing BSTs at every node vs. update cost

