- How to handle frequent deletions and stale BST entries efficiently?
- Could a Fenwick/segment-tree variant work if you assign a global index to words by lexicographic order?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates combined data structures and time-based eviction. Tests ability to design per-key streaming aggregates with real-time top-K, using arrays/linked lists/hashtable/heap and lazy deletions.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window aggregation; - Hash maps for per-tag buckets; - Deques for per-tag event queues; - Max-heap with lazy invalidation for top-K.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingTagTopK {
  constructor(windowMs){ this.W=windowMs; this.tagMap=new Map(); this.heap=[]; this.ver=0; }
  _expire(tag, now){ const b=this.tagMap.get(tag); while (b.queue.length && b.queue[0].ts <= now - this.W){ b.sum -= b.queue.shift().val; } }
  addEvent(tag, userId, value, ts){ let b=this.tagMap.get(tag); if(!b){ b={sum:0, queue:[], ver:0}; this.tagMap.set(tag,b);} this._expire(tag, ts); b.queue.push({ts, val: value}); b.sum += value; this.ver++; // push new entry
    this._heapPush({sum: b.sum, tag, ver:this.ver}); }
  topK(K){ // lazy
    const res=[]; const seen=new Set(); while (res.length<K && this.heap.length){ const top=this._heapPop(); if (top.ver===this.tagMap.get(top.tag).ver){ res.push(top.tag); seen.add(top.tag); } }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or varying window sizes per tag?
- How would you optimize memory and support concurrent producers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about multi-key sorting, stability, and practical trade-offs in beginner-friendly tasks. It also aligns with real-world data processing where groupings matter.

## Key Concepts

- Sorting stability and its impact on multi-key ordering
- Two-pass stable sorting technique
- Time complexity: O(n log n) for mergesort
- Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary memory

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableMergeSortBy(arr, key) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2)
  const left = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(0, mid), key)
  const right = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(mid), key)
  const out = []
  let i = 0, j = 0
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i][key] <= right[j][key]) out.push(left[i++])
    else out.push(right[j++])
  }
  return out.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j))
}

function sortRecords(arr) {
  const byTime = stableMergeSortBy(arr, 'time')
  return stableMergeSortBy(byTime, 'priority')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify if you must sort in place and the library sort is not stable?
- If time values span a large range, how could you optimize space or use counting/radix techniques to reduce complexity?

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicate values are common in real-world data sets. Traditional 2-way quicksort performs poorly with many duplicates, leading to unnecessary comparisons and degraded performance. 3-way partitioning addresses this by grouping equal elements together, significantly reducing the algorithm's work.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag algorithm)**: Separates array into < pivot, == pivot, and > pivot regions
- **Randomized pivot selection**: Prevents worst-case O(n²) scenarios on already sorted or adversarial input
- **In-place sorting**: Operates with O(1) additional space, using only O(log n) expected stack depth
- **Performance optimization**: Reduces comparisons from O(n log n) to O(n) when many duplicates exist

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  // Random pivot selection
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;

  // 3-way partitioning
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++;
      i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++; // a[i] === pivot
    }
  }

  // Recursively sort subarrays
  quicksort3(a, lo, lt - 1);
  quicksort3(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Partitioning Strategy

The algorithm maintains three regions during partitioning:
- `[lo, lt-1]`: elements < pivot
- `[lt, gt]`: elements === pivot (being processed)
- `[gt+1, hi]`: elements > pivot

As we iterate, elements are swapped into their appropriate region, with equal elements naturally forming the middle section that needs no further processing.

## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals, algorithm selection, and space-time tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
**BFS (Breadth-First Search)**: Uses queue, explores neighbors level-by-level. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(V) worst case (all nodes in queue). Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs.

**DFS (Depth-First Search)**: Uses stack or recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(h) where h is maximum depth (recursion stack), O(V) worst case for skewed graphs.

## When to Use Each
**BFS**: Shortest path routing, social network friend suggestions, web crawlers finding closest pages, level-order tree traversals.

**DFS**: Topological sorting, cycle detection, maze solving, finding connected components, backtracking problems like Sudoku or N-Queens.

## Code Example
```javascript
// BFS - finds shortest path
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const distances = { [start]: 0 };

  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        distances[neighbor] = distances[node] + 1;
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return distances;
}

// DFS - recursive implementation
function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set(), result = []) {
  if (visited.has(node)) return result;

  visited.add(node);
  result.push(node);

  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, result);
  }

  return result;
}

// DFS - iterative implementation
function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;

    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node);

    // Add neighbors to stack (reverse for consistent order)
    for (let i = graph[node].length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const neighbor = graph[node][i];
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        stack.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Memory Considerations
BFS can consume significant memory for wide graphs (queue holds all nodes at current level). DFS is memory-efficient for deep, narrow graphs but may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion. Choose based on graph structure and problem requirements.

